   - "Workflow" section: per task, Muse Spark 1.3 Standard (`muse-spark-1.3`) designs (`docs/tasks/NN-name.md`) and reviews (`docs/reviews/NN.md`); `muse-spark-1.3-contributor` implements. Up to 2 implement/review rounds. A rescue by another tool only after that, logged in `RESCUE_LOG.md` with date, task, tool, why Muse got stuck, what changed, and commits, with a `Rescue: <tool>` trailer. Save every Muse Code session's JSONL in `agent-logs/NN-<step>.jsonl`. Every commit carries the Muse Code co-author trailer (added by the hook). Update `BUILT_DURING_HACKATHON.md` with each task.
   - "Lectoria rules": text is divided into scenes (not paragraphs or chapters); paragraph ids are global like `c12p34`; emotions taxonomy (8): calm, joy, sadness, suspense, mystery, adventure, combat, wonder, plus `unknown` on failure (never fake a fallback emotion); public-domain books only; images follow the book text and W. W. Denslow's 1900 illustrations (silver shoes, nothing from the 1939 MGM film); never copy code from github.com/martinmw13/lectoria; cheapest models while testing (Contributor), Standard for the final pass and the public chat.
   - "Security rules" (summary, link to SECURITY.md): secrets only in env vars (`.env.local` locally, gitignored; Vercel env vars in prod); `.env.example` has names only; never `NEXT_PUBLIC_` for a key; any module touching META/JEV/Baseten/Modal/Upstash keys starts with `import 'server-only'`; the pre-commit secret scan must pass and is never bypassed with `--no-verify`; errors to the client are generic, details only in server logs and never with keys; never log request headers.
   - "Writing rules": all docs, UI copy and commit messages are unslopped with the `unslop` skill: no filler, no hype, no emoji headers, no words like seamless, elevate, delve, robust, leverage. Sentence-case headings. Commit messages: imperative subject under 72 chars, body says why.
